  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three strategies for making electrical interfaces with the inside of a cell. In all cases, the cell membrane is the key barrier, and each approach overcomes it in a different way.\n\nOne method uses endocytosed electrodes: mushroom-like structures anchored on a substrate are engulfed by the cell membrane, so the cell wraps around them and forms a close internalized contact. A second approach uses cell-penetrating nanoprobes, shown as nanoscale posts or tubes that directly cross the lipid bilayer to access the intracellular space from the substrate below. This emphasizes a more localized, penetrating entry into the cell rather than membrane wrapping.\n\nThe third strategy uses a field-effect transistor geometry. A nano-FET spans the membrane, with source and drain on opposite sides, so the device senses changes associated with the intracellular environment through the membrane-crossing element. The schematic highlights the distinction between intracellular and extracellular space more explicitly here.\n\nOverall, the image summarizes different nanoelectronic designs for intracellular recording or sensing: membrane engulfment, direct penetration, and transistor-based detection across the membrane.",
